PATH3 at GIF packet granularity: PATH3 data that arrives while VIF1's MSKPATH3 masks PATH3 is split into
EOP-terminated GIF packets; clearing the mask releases one packet (the GIF finishes the packet it started before
a new MSKPATH3 takes effect), and the rest flow once the VIF1 stream carries on with PATH3 unmasked. Released
packets take submitGifPacket's route, so they stay in order with PATH1/PATH2 on the VU/GIF workers.
Why: Jackass's RenderWare texture cache sends a frame's ~50 texture uploads as one ~950 KB GIF-channel chain
while PATH3 is masked, then opens PATH3 for one texture at a time (FLUSHA; MSKPATH3 0; MSKPATH3 1) right before
the draws that use it, reusing a ring of GS memory (0x2BC0-0x3680). The runtime queued the chain as one blob and
released all of it at the first unmask, so later uploads wrapped the ring and overwrote textures and palettes
that pending draws still sampled: noise on the main and Story Mode menus, purple/green terrain in stunts.
PS2X_PATH3_PACKETS=0 restores the old behaviour. Also PS2X_GIF_PATH_LOG=<seconds>:<count>, a log of GIF
submissions (path, size, first tag, mask) and VIF1 MSKPATH3/FLUSH/FLUSHA, for finding this kind of ordering bug.
Test: tests/path3_packets_test.cpp (cmake --build build/linux --target ja_path3_packets_test).
Origin: this project (jackass-ps2-recomp), 2026-11-07.

